{
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "fact": "As of July 15, 2016, the UK Foreign Office rated the terror threat level in France as 'high' due to ongoing threats from Islamist terrorist groups and recent attacks in Paris and Nice.",
      "source_date": "2016-07-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "fact": "As of July 15, 2016, the UK Foreign Office rated the terror threat level in Turkey as 'high' due to attacks by Kurdish groups, Daesh, and far-left organizations.",
      "source_date": "2016-07-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "fact": "As of July 15, 2016, the UK Foreign Office rated the terror threat level in Belgium as 'high' following the Brussels bombings in March 2016.",
      "source_date": "2016-07-15"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "fact": "In 2015, Egypt experienced a significant decline in tourism following the downing of Metrojet Flight 9268 in October 2015 and a series of terror attacks, leading to a sharp drop in visitor numbers.",
      "source_date": "2015-11-01"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "fact": "In early 2016, the UK Foreign Office advised against all travel to the Sinai Peninsula (except Sharm El Sheikh airport) and advised against all but essential travel to other parts of Egypt due to the threat of terrorism.",
      "source_date": "2016-01-20"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "fact": "In 2015, total tourist arrivals to Egypt were approximately 9.5 million, a significant drop from pre-2011 levels, with monthly averages well below 1 million in many months due to security concerns.",
      "source_date": "2016-01-15"
    }
  ],
  "reasoning": "Based on the evidence available before September 2016, the global security environment was characterized by high terror threats in major tourist destinations like France, Turkey, and Belgium [1, 2, 3]. Egypt specifically faced severe security challenges, with the UK Foreign Office advising against all travel to large parts of the country in early 2016 due to terrorism risks [5]. The tourism sector in Egypt had already suffered a massive collapse in 2015 following the Metrojet crash and other attacks, with total annual arrivals dropping to around 9.5 million [4, 6]. Given the persistent high threat level and the advisory against travel, it was highly improbable that Egypt would see a surge to over 1 million tourists in a single month (October 2016) so soon after the 2015 crash, especially when the monthly average in 2015 was significantly lower. The actual data for October 2016 (506,000 arrivals) confirms this trend, but the prediction must rely on the pre-cutoff context of high risk and low recovery.",
  "probability_yes": 0.05,
  "prediction": "no"
}

